NEW YORK (Reuters) - An agreement allowing Ukraine to safely export grain across the Black Sea last year expires on Monday (July 17) after Russia said it would not join the agreement. No more this. This is according to the release by the AFP news agency on July 17, 2023.
The agreement, negotiated by the United Nations and Turkey in July last year, aims to alleviate the global food crisis by allowing safe grain exports to Ukraine, which has been hampered by the Russia-Ukraine conflict.
He stressed that the decision not to renew the agreement was not related to the attack on the bridge between Russia and Crimea, which he called "terrorism" and blamed Ukraine.
Russia has repeatedly threatened to withdraw from the deal, complaining that its demands for a grain export agreement have not been met. Russia also alleges that the grain exported did not reach poor countries as claimed by stakeholders.
However, the United Nations said the agreement benefited those countries by helping to reduce food prices by more than 20 percent worldwide.
