In an interview with Russian state television broadcast on Sunday, Russian President Vladimir Putin said: "Russia has a sufficient number of cluster munitions. "If they are used against us, then we have the right to take appropriate action."
Putin claims that Russia has not used cluster munitions in Ukraine. However, Human Rights Watch alleges that both Ukraine and Russia have already used similar weapons on the battlefield. This week, Ukraine said it had begun receiving cluster munitions from the United States and had promised to use them only in areas with a large concentration of enemy forces.
Humanitarian groups have strongly condemned the United States' decision to provide cluster munitions to Ukraine, as the controversial weapon is banned by 111 countries party to the Cluster Munitions Convention. The United States, Russia and Ukraine are not parties to the convention. Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng: People who prevent people from going to the polls and instruct to mark the ballot is illegal
