General Knowledge: How did the Guam Islands belong to the United States?
Guam is nearly 10,000 kilometers from the United States. The only reason the United States came to occupy the Guam was because the United States was at war with Spain. When the US-Spanish War broke out in April 1898, the island of Guam was still under Spanish control, dating back to the 1600s.
Finally, when both sides understood that they were enemies, the United States asked the Spanish governor of Guam to surrender, giving it 30 minutes. The Spanish governor agreed and Guam became US territory. The U.S. military has been there for just over a day and has left no one in charge of Guam and has even returned the flag.
It was the first and last event between the two sides during the US-Spanish War that took place on the Guam and there was no bloodshed. When the United States won the war, Guam became its official territory, and the people on this island, which the US government calls Gammon, are now American citizens by birth.
