Top Chinese diplomat urges stable ties with India
According to the Chinese Ministry of Foreign Affairs on the sidelines of the ASEAN Summit in Jakarta, Indonesia, Wang said China and India must work in the same direction to find a mutually acceptable border solution.
India and China share a 3,800-kilometer border, having been in conflict since 1962. In 1990, relations between the two countries improved after a series of border agreements, and China is now India's second largest trading partner.
However, in 2020, 20 Indian and four Chinese soldiers were killed in hand-to-hand combat during border clashes, prompting both armies to deploy troops and military equipment. Several rounds of military and diplomatic talks helped ease tensions between the two armies, but New Delhi described the situation on the border as fragile and dangerous.
