Royal University of Phnom Penh students expect to contribute to the restoration of tourism after graduation
(Phnom Penh): Tourism Management students at the Royal University of Phnom Penh (RUPP) are expected to contribute to the promotion and recovery of tourism after the Kovid-19 crisis after graduation, as tourism is considered an important priority. In building the socio-economy.
This was stated by Ms. Chip Hunleap after she defended the 20th bachelor's thesis for tourism management students organized by the Department of Tourism of the Royal University of Phnom Penh on the morning of July 15, 2023 This.
She said that her university education in her generation was at a time when the world was facing the Kovid-19 crisis and tourism was also severely affected, but she did not give up studying this subject because she understood that this subject Will be able to help address the impact on tourism during this global crisis.
Ms. Khun Chanmony, Head of the Department of Tourism of the Royal University of Phnom Penh (RUPP), emphasized that even during the Kovid-19 crisis, students in this specialty did not decline because they had their own perspectives combined with their belief in The leadership of the Royal Government, until they graduate, this global epidemic will be manageable and obviously so.
Tourism is a wide range of job options in government institutions, private companies, organizations or starting your own business. Therefore, this study is really suitable for the general context, especially in the recovery phase of tourism is a good opportunity for post-graduate students to find work and contribute to the Royal Government in attracting national and international tourism.
"In general, the development of tourism is possible only if the country is peaceful and there is no war and no infectious diseases," she said. If we keep the peace, he will come. Take a look at Cambodia, under the right leadership of the Royal Government, we have peace and we can control infectious diseases. "So our tourism sector will grow and all tourism students will be a part of contributing to the restoration of tourism."
