The World War I Memorial is located at the intersection of Monivong Blvd., France Blvd., Oknha Techo Blvd. and the Cannon Roundabout, Srah Chak Commune, Daun Penh District, Phnom Penh.
In 1975, the monument commemorating those who died in World War I was bombed by the Khmer Rouge, leaving only a few fragments, such as elephant heads, lions, dragons, and bells, were preserved. Phnom Penh National Museum.
The monument was rebuilt by Dr. Ly Raksmey, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Land Management, Urban Planning and Construction in collaboration with the Ministry of Culture and Fine Arts and the Phnom Penh Capital Administration. The monument was built in the style of Paul Ducuing.
Dr. Ly Raksmey, an architect, said that the monument commemorating those who died in World War I was a co-style architecture that incorporated many traditional Khmer architectural concepts from the Angkorian period to the post-Angkorian period. The most special point is that this monument has a lot of the same view as the Neak Poan temple, with dragons, elephants and lions all in the same direction.
Dr. Ly Raksmey, the architect, also highlighted that the reconstruction of this monument is based on two values. The first is a reminder of the heroic deeds of Cambodian and French warriors who sacrificed their lives in World War I. The names of all these revered individuals are inscribed on a solid rock that lasts for a long time. Respectively.
And the continuation of the Khmer tradition, the attention of the French government and the Khmer king, to warm the hearts of the families of the soldiers who sacrificed their lives in the war. Honorary works of all those heroes as well.
The following is information related to construction:
* The technical team removed the remaining metal fragments from the fracture and reassembled them to preserve the preservation and historical value.
* Khoeun Vimean: Built of concrete and granite in the shape of an octagon, size 14.20 meters by 14.20 meters, height 1.70 meters, there is a flower garden at the bottom of Khoeun, the same as the foot of Hempen Mountain, next to Khoeun The three-tiered cone resembles Mount Sumeru in Khmer architecture, a place inhabited by angels. The three-tiered cone arrangement has been present in Cambodia since at least the 7th century.
* Four Lion Guards: Beautifully decorated with various designs on the corner of the monument, modeled on the Khmer art form of Angkor in the 12th century AD. Source: Preah Thkol temple, Preah Vihear province.
* Neak Chrung Anutis: Four dragons modeled on the Khmer art form of the Angkorian period, 12th century AD. Located at the corner of the top of the elephant's head and below the bell. Looks like it is surrounded by bells. The dragon design on the elephant along the sub-corner of the palace is a synthesis of personal views incorporated into the building.
* Statue of Khmer-French soldiers: 2.80 meters high, with the French left hand on the shoulder of the Cambodian soldier, right hand holding a laurel leaf, while the Cambodian soldier with both hands raised on the laurel leaf as a symbol of peace. Both statues represent the heroism, courage and dedication that led to the victory in 1918 in France and symbolize the historical friendship, solidarity and cooperation between Cambodia and France.
