Philippines uses prisoners to work in agriculture to boost food production as country faces food insecurity
The Philippines will convert criminal property across the country into farmland and train prisoners to grow agricultural crops to boost food production, the Philippine government said on Thursday. This is according to the release by the AFP news agency on July 13, 2023.
The project, called the Sustainable Environment Reform Initiative for Food Security (RISE), aims to use and rehabilitate farmland in prisons and criminal farms across the country to boost food production towards food security and sustainability. .
The Philippines, with a population of nearly 110 million, faces food insecurity, especially for low-income people due to persistent malnutrition and starvation. The potential impact of this year's El Nino phenomenon has further threatened domestic food production.
