China to reward those who provide information on illegal mines
BEIJING: The China Rare Mining Industry Association said on Tuesday it would provide cash rewards for information on mining, processing and smuggling of rare earths. According to Reuters.
Reuters reported, citing a press release issued by the China Rare Mining Industry Association, that the association would review the information submitted and send the information to the relevant authorities if necessary.
Chinese authorities have taken a number of measures to try to curb long-standing problems with illegal mining in recent years, including the annual mining quota system. Globally increasing.
Data from the United States Geological Survey (USGS) show that China will dominate the rare earths market, accounting for 70 percent of global rare earth production by 2022.
