Commentary: The Asia-Pacific region is a favorite for cooperation and development, but not a chessboard for competing powers.
BEIJING (Reuters) - A NATO summit opened in Lithuania on July 11, local time. In addition to key issues on the situation in Ukraine, the leaders of Japan, South Korea, Australia and New Zealand attended the meeting for the second year in a row, turning NATO's focus on the Asia-Pacific region. Heat again.
According to Japanese media reports, due to French opposition, NATO plans to open its first Asia liaison office in Japan, which is scheduled to be discussed at the summit, have been postponed until Post-autumn period. But analysts believe that under US guidance, NATO's intention to expand its framework of influence in the Asia-Pacific region will not change.
