The Philippine Armed Forces reported on Friday that it had spotted 48 ships believed to belong to the "China Marine Corps" during an air patrol in late June. The ships were spotted in the Iroquois Reef, which Philippine authorities claim as its exclusive economic zone.
Philippine Coast Guard spokesman Jay Tarriela said in a radio interview: "Based on our latest inspections, there are still Chinese maritime militias present in the area, so the Philippine Coast Guard and "The Philippine Armed Forces will step up patrols next week to ensure they leave the reef."
In mid-April, more than 100 Chinese ships were found in Philippine waters in the South China Sea, including the Chinese Marines and two Chinese Coast Guard vessels.
China claims sovereignty over almost all of the South China Sea based on the so-called "Nine Lines", which extend more than 1,500 kilometers from mainland China and cut into the country's exclusive economic zone. Many, including the Philippines.
The International Court of Justice in The Hague rejected China's claim in 2016, but Beijing did not recognize the ruling and has rapidly developed its military presence in recent years, including the construction of an artificial island base in the waters. Controversial.
CMAC fully supports Samdech Techo's call for US and Ukrainian presidents not to use cluster munitions, Cambodia has been hit by about 30 million cluster munitions
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Heng Ratana, the Royal Government Delegate in charge of the Director General of the Cambodian Mine Action Center (CMAC), a well-known unit in charge of clearing mines and unexploded ordnance in Cambodia, has announced his full support. In response to a message from Cambodian Prime Minister Samdech Techo Hun Sen urging the US and Ukrainian presidents not to use cluster munitions in the Russia-Ukraine war.
He said that cluster munitions are the remnants of the war in Cambodia, even though they are more than 50 years old, they still continue to threaten the lives and safety of the Cambodian people.
The pain and hardships of the war, especially the cluster munitions in Cambodia and some of the affected countries, were also part of the movement to establish the Oslo International Convention on the Prohibition of the Use, Production, Stockpiling and Transport of Cluster Munitions. Entered into force under international law in 2008, there are 123 countries, including 111 States Parties and 12 signatories.
He stressed that in the 1960s and 1970s, Cambodia had dropped about 30 million cluster munitions on tens of thousands of targets across the country. Studies have shown that cluster munitions used in the Indochina War had a non-explosive rate of about 25 to 30 percent in warfare, a quantity that continued to be dangerous in the long run.
He added that the leaders of Britain, Canada, Germany, Spain, Austria and other civil society organizations had reportedly opposed the decision to provide and use "cluster munitions" in the Russo-Ukrainian war.
Please be informed that on July 9, 2023, the Prime Minister of Cambodia, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, called on the President of the United States and the President of Ukraine not to use cluster munitions, as it is the greatest danger for the people of Ukraine for decades or even hundreds of years. More.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen's call came after reports that the United States would supply cluster munitions to Ukraine to fight the Russian army.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen's message read: "These days, there is a lot of news that the United States will provide cluster munitions to Ukraine to fight the Russian army. If this is true, it could be the greatest danger to the Ukrainian people for decades or even hundreds of years if the cluster munitions were used in Russian-occupied territory.
