The International Atomic Energy Agency (IAEA) recently approved Tokyo's plan to release treated water from the Fukushima nuclear plant, which was damaged by the tsunami. But the plan has raised concerns in neighboring countries such as China, which has decided to ban the import of certain food products from Japan, as well as sparking protests in South Korea.
In a statement released by the state-run KCNA media, a North Korean official said the discharge from the Fukushima nuclear plant would have a serious impact on human life as well as security and environmental ecosystems. The official added that there was no reason for the IAEA to allow Japan to release the water into the sea.
Both the government and the International Atomic Energy Agency (IAEA) have ensured that the treated water from the Fukushima nuclear plant has minimal impact. Mr. Neth Pheaktra: To vote for the Cambodian People's Party is to vote for yourself, vote for peace and development
