Recent findings underscore the importance of Virachey National Park for this endangered species.
A previous study by the Wildlife and Plant Organization with the Ministry of Environment in 2021 found the presence of female and male cubs in Virachey National Park, indicating that the species has reproduced in the area.
Further findings of this species clearly indicate that this rare species is living in this national park area. However, the species has been at risk of extinction due to threats from poaching and trapping.
