China's central bank's gold holdings rose 680,000 trillion ounces last month, Bloomberg reported, citing official data released on Friday. This amount is equal to 23 tons. China's current total gold reserves are 2,330 tonnes, with an additional 183 tonnes purchased since November.
China is a gold-buying country, in part because of its desire to reduce the strength of the dollar in world markets. China is also struggling with a disappointing economic recovery from epidemics and strained relations with the United States.
Central banks around the world bought an unprecedented amount of gold last year, accounting for nearly a quarter of global demand, amid rising inflation and tightening monetary policy. According to Reuters.
The signing will take place at a ministerial meeting that is a member of the Comprehensive and Progressive Partnership Agreement, Bloomberg reported, citing a press release issued by the New Zealand Ministry of Foreign Affairs and Trade. Trans-Pacific (CPTPP) organized by New Zealand. The event is scheduled for July 15-16.
The past CPTPP agreement has been seen as a way to balance China's growing influence in the Asia-Pacific region. Former President Donald Trump pulled the United States out of the deal in 2017, narrowing the scope of the deal and leaving Japan as the largest economy in the deal. The Tokyo government is pressuring the United States to return.
The current members of the agreement include Australia, Brunei, Canada, Chile, Japan, Malaysia, Mexico, New Zealand, Peru, Singapore and Vietnam, Bloomberg reports. The bloc, which has a population of 500 million, will account for 15% of the world's GDP when the UK joins, according to the International Monetary Fund.
China is the next country to join the agreement, having applied just after Britain in 2021, followed by Taiwan, Ecuador, Costa Rica, Uruguay and Ukraine.
