US finally destroys its chemical weapons
WASHINGTON (Reuters) - US President Joe Biden on Friday announced that the United States has completely destroyed all chemical weapons in its arsenal. This was released by the AFP news agency on Saturday, July 8, 2023.
"Today, I am very proud to announce that the United States has safely dismantled the last remaining chemical weapons in its arsenal, as this brings us closer to a world," Biden said. "There is no longer a fear of chemical weapons."
The Chemical Weapons Convention, which was agreed in 1993 and entered into force in 1997, has given the United States until September 30 this year to completely dismantle its chemical weapons. The other signatories have already demolished their chemical weapons, a total of 72,000 tons since the entry into force.
Countries that have not signed or ratified the CWC include Egypt, Israel, North Korea and South Sudan, while four signatories - Myanmar, Iran, Russia and Syria - are suspected of possessing chemical weapons. Not announced publicly. Dr. Soeng Samnang continued to meet with people in three communes in Kampong Leng district to seek votes for the Cambodian People's Party.
