In the cause of upholding national cultural heritage and defending national sovereignty, the Prime Minister of Cambodia, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, sent a letter to UNESCO in 2001 requesting the registration of the Temple of Preah Vihear as a World Heritage Site. The Prime Minister said that the inscription of the Temple of Preah Vihear on the World Heritage List was in line with the planned plan, which is a priority of Cambodia's foreign policy.
The Royal Government of Cambodia, led by Samdech Techo, with special envoys such as Samdech Vibol Panha Sok An, has worked hard to prepare joint documents and other procedures in accordance with UNESCO requirements. Just days after the temple was inscribed on the World Heritage List, Thailand raised troops to invade Cambodian territory in the area of the Preah Vihear temple.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the head of the Royal Government and a Khmer Rouge son who was determined to defend the nation and the territory, fought hard by all means, including the armed forces and the means of international law, to protect this valuable property of Cambodia from being lost. .
