General knowledge: The meaning of the four faces at Bayon temple
(Phnom Penh): Bayon temple in the center of Angkor Thom, which was built to dedicate to Mahayana Buddhism "Bodhisattva Avalokitesvara" and for the royal temple of Jayavarman VII.
The architecture is in the form of four towers, with each face having a beautiful smile and the eyes seem to be looking down. At this point, there are two different views: the first view is said to represent the faces of the bodhisattva Avalokitesvara, and the second view is said to be the face of Jayavarman VII, the founder. Build this temple.
2. These four faces of Jayavarman VII also represent the four Brahma temples of Jayavarman VII, which are available to the people: compassion, devotion, and devotion, which he is constantly concerned about. Of the people more than his body. According to an inscription from his reign, the suffering of the people hurt the king twice as much.
Indeed, during his reign, up to 102 hospitals and 121 guesthouses were built throughout the empire, especially along the main ancient national roads leading all the way from Angkor to provide medical treatment and Provides temporary shelter during which travelers pass without discrimination on the basis of class or status (Michel Trane).
