JAKARTA: The economic recovery after a devastating disease has pushed Indonesia back to a high-middle-income country once again, paving the way for the country to achieve its national status goals. High income. According to Reuters.
According to the latest World Bank (WB) rating, Indonesia regained its ranking this year as per capita national income rose to $ 4,580. This level of income is up from the previous figure of $ 4,140, which will keep Indonesia as a low-middle-income country for two consecutive years by 2022.
The World Bank (WB) noted that "Indonesia has continued to recover after the epidemic and its gross domestic product has risen by 5.3 percent." The country's GDP in 2022 is growing at the fastest pace in nine years.
