Of the more than 550 people who ventured into space, only three died on the spot, the former Soviet Union astronauts during a 1971 mission.
Over the next three years, the Apollo astronauts accomplished seven more missions, including the first Apollo 11 landing on the moon and the troubled Apollo 13 mission in the middle of the moon. In space. On June 30, 1971, humanity experienced the first case of loss of life in space and the only case to date.
They spent three weeks on it, not only breaking the record for the longest stay in space (as of then), they also conducted several experiments that focused on On how long-term stay in space can have an effect on the human body.
Investigators believe it was caused by a broken safety valve that burst into the vehicle they were riding in to land. At an altitude of 168 km, the air pressure in the trunk of the three vehicles was quickly absorbed and they could not find a solution.
Eventually, they also suffocate to death in space, on the Kaman line, which is recognized as the boundary line between the Earth's atmosphere and space. As a result of this tragedy, the Soviet Union changed its policy, requiring all astronauts to wear safety suits during the space shuttle return to Earth, and Russia continues to apply this principle to this day and no such accidents occur again.
