(Phnom Penh): On Wednesday, the 11th day of the first month of the year Thao Panh Sak 2567 On June 28, 2023, tomorrow, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, continues to meet with a total of 18,313 workers from 17 factories operating in Phnom Penh. The meeting took place at a plot of land in Prey Speu area, Khan Por Senchey, Phnom Penh.
Phnom Penh, the heart of Cambodia, is a potential trade and investment center under government policy. In 2023, Phnom Penh has 9,314 factories with more than 660,000 workers with an average wage of about 2 billion US dollars per year. There are 727 garment factories with a total of more than 339,000 workers with an average wage of 1.5 billion US dollars per year.
Tomorrow's meeting is the 17th meeting for the second round. The 17 meetings had a total of 314,000 workers. Samdech Techo is scheduled to meet two more times, which will meet a total of 25,000 workers.
According to a report by the Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training, in 1997 there were only 64 garment factories in Cambodia with about 80,000 workers and exports of about 227 million US dollars.
UN refugee agency says more than 1 million people may have fled Sudan conflict
GENEVA: The United Nations High Commissioner for Refugees (UNHCR) warned on Tuesday that earlier estimates that the conflict in Sudan would force 1 million people to flee across the border, but could actually go beyond that. This is according to the release by the AFP news agency on Tuesday, June 27, 2023.
Clashes between the army and paramilitary forces that have been fighting since mid-April have so far forced nearly 600,000 people to flee to neighboring countries, including Egypt, Chad, South Sudan and the Republic. Central Africa.
However, he did not specify the number of refugees he estimated to have fled the country to more than 1 million.
The United Nations estimates that more than 2.5 million people have been displaced since April, most of them internally displaced in Sudan.
Pakistan Parliament approves draft law paving the way for exiled former Prime Minister Sharif to return to politics
ISLAMABAD: A Pakistani parliament has approved a draft law that would set a deadline for the removal of lawmakers, allowing those who have been deprived of their rights for life to return, a government spokesman said on Tuesday. Re-run for public office. This is according to the release by the AFP news agency on Tuesday, June 27, 2023.
A government spokesman said the acting president had signed an amendment to the law stating that the court could only remove members of parliament for no more than five years.
Sharif has served as Pakistan's prime minister three times, most recently before he was ousted in 2017 on corruption charges.
