Minister of Agriculture supports the initiative of agricultural contracts between companies and farmers to help solve market problems for farmers
(Kampong Thom): The Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, Mr. Dith Tina, affirmed that the initiative to have an agricultural contract between rice companies, rice mills and farmers' communities is a mechanism to contribute to solving market problems for farmers.
For example, on June 27, 2023, a total of 6 large companies and rice mills signed contracts to purchase nearly 20,000 tons of rice and vegetables according to the actual harvest from 40 farming communities throughout Kampong Thom province. A Memorandum of Understanding (MoU) was signed with a microfinance institution to guarantee payment at harvest time to procure nearly 20,000 tons of rice and vegetables according to the actual harvest.
"This agricultural contract initiative is designed to help solve market problems for farmers," said Minister Dith Tina.
He added that the signing of agricultural contracts requires all parties, both exporters, mills and communities to respect the contract and be honest with each other.
The Minister also urged the community to use pure seeds and apply the right techniques as the Ministry is preparing a new agricultural system with a policy of placing 1,600 agricultural technical officers in all communes with agricultural activities and considerations. Community, both technically and financially.
However, Mr. Dith Tina stressed that this work requires the participation of all stakeholders in the agricultural sector with transparency, accountability and high responsibility.
Mr. Chan Sokheang pointed out that these 46 contracts cover an area of 6,690 hectares and are implemented by 2,236 farmers in seven districts of Kampong Thom province.
The annual signing ceremony is a symbol of increasing trust, confidence and encouraging the farming community and companies in Cambodia to continue to participate in and further enhance this important agricultural contract mechanism. Strengthen relationships and create markets for farming communities, mill owners and exporters.
