(Koh Kong): In general, forests have two functions, the first is the product and the second is the function, in this sense, the forest provides non-timber forest products for medicinal use, etc. In addition, it also has the function of absorbing groundwater to prevent erosion. And especially the absorption of carbon dioxide from the atmosphere, which helps us to sell carbon for socio-economic development.
This was confirmed by Mr. Chuob Paris, Undersecretary of State of the Ministry of Environment in the gathering of environmental knowledge on climate change and REDD + in Cambodia with journalists on Monday, June 26, 2023 at the Department of Environment, Koh Kong Province.
Mr. Chuob Paris stated that the Royal Government of Cambodia, through the Ministry of Environment, pays close attention to the management of natural resources and the environment, including the preservation of timber to sell the role of forests, which play an important role in protecting the planet's ecosystem. Term sale of carbon credit.
He added that Cambodia is participating in international efforts to reduce climate change and reduce greenhouse gas emissions, while pursuing other national goals for society and the environment.
At the same time, he stressed that the UN Framework Convention on Climate Change encourages countries that have worked hard to better protect their forests to raise funds through the sale of carbon credits.
The senior official of the Ministry of Environment also said that due to the efforts to better manage and conserve protected areas, Cambodia is renewing its carbon credit agreement in the coming years.
Mr. Ken Serey Ratha, Director General of the General Department of Environmental Knowledge and Information and Director of the REDD + National Secretariat, said that since 2016, Cambodia has received funding from the sale of carbon credits in two projects at the Keo Seima Wildlife Sanctuary. Mondulkiri Province and South Cardamom Mountain National Park, Koh Kong Province.
According to Mr. Khvay Atiya, Undersecretary of State of the Ministry of Environment and Coordinator of the program, this is the second time that the environmental awareness program has been organized especially with journalists to strengthen the capacity to disseminate environmental information better. Natural resource management, biodiversity conservation and sustainable livelihoods of local people.
Journalists gain environmental knowledge through the sharing of leaders and experts of the Ministry of Environment regarding the strengthening of the national economy and the family economy of the people at the local level, the efforts of the Royal Government and the Ministry of Environment to implement the program. And strategies such as REDD +, carbon credit projects, and community livelihood development.
Mr. Hun Mardy, Director of Koh Kong Provincial Department of Environment Red Plus, as a mechanism, has provided a global fund to better support the conservation of forests in the Cardamom Mountains and other protected areas in Koh Kong Province. In addition, it also contributes to the sustainable development of local communities, such as providing water tanks, clean water systems, and building some local infrastructure.
It should be noted that the Global Knowledge Awareness Program on Climate Change and the National REDD + Program in Cambodia with Journalists was organized for 3 days from 25 to 27 June 2023 by the Ministry of Environment, coordinated by the Department of Environment of Koh Kong Province. Supported by the Wildlife Alliance and has 20 journalists (from radio, television and electronic media).
