Government officials, local authorities and conservationists: a peaceful country is a time when we need to provide peace and security for wildlife
(Battambang): Cambodia under the umbrella of peace, political stability throughout the country has allowed all citizens to have all rights in accordance with the law, while forests and natural resources must also be properly protected under the law. Natural resources. But there are still some threats posed by human factors.
The Ministry of Environment, on behalf of the staff of the Royal Government and its officials, has the responsibility in ownership to fulfill its roles and responsibilities on the protection and conservation of natural resources on the basis of the law in force.
Mr. Net Pheaktra stated that natural resources, wildlife and humans all play an important role for the planet. So if any biodiversity is lost, the ecosystem balance will be lost. If biodiversity is not protected, our food will be lost. He said that eating wild animals is the elimination of their own food sources because nature provides food for all people.
"Leading this zero-trap campaign to provide wildlife safety, why do we talk about wildlife safety? When we talk about safety for humans, wildlife also needs animal safety," the spokesman said. "Forests also need peace. Wildlife also needs shelter to be able to reproduce."
In addition, Mr. Net Pheaktra said that the loss of wildlife will make people lose everything, so we do not want to see only the forest, but not the presence of wildlife. The presence of wildlife is part of attracting tourists for income to the local community.
Mr. Pheng Sethy, Deputy Governor of Battambang Province, said that the provincial authorities are committed to eradicating traps, which are destroying valuable wildlife for Battambang province.
Mr. Pheng Sethy said that together in the forest of Battambang province, there must be no more traps, and together in the restaurants, there must be no sale of food made from wild animals.
According to Kong Monichan, Director of the MJP Foundation in Cambodia, there are many rare species of wildlife in the study area. While the area is loved by Angelina Jolie, Hollywood star, owner of the Madej Julie Pitt Foundation (MJP), which was established in 2003 and expanded its branch in Cambodia in 2006, has invested in the protection and conservation of wildlife and natural resources at the resort.
The Director of the MJP Foundation in Cambodia has called for joint efforts to overcome obstacles to the protection of forests and wildlife in heavily populated areas, and that campaigns related to the protection and conservation of natural resources should not be stopped by the second phase of the Zero Trap Campaign. More needs to be done to make the forest and wildlife as peaceful as humans.
