Senior official of the Ministry of Commerce: The CPP is working with national and international organizations in the private sector to develop regulations on the minimum age for those who can consume alcohol.
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Phan Oun, Representative of the Royal Government of Cambodia, in charge of the Director General of the General Department of Consumer Protection, Competition and Anti-Fraud (KPR) of the Ministry of Commerce, emphasized that the General Department (KPR) ) Has been working with national and international organizations as well as the private sector to develop minimum age regulations for those who can purchase and consume alcohol in order to protect underage youth.
Mr. Phan Oun said that currently the Royal Government as well as the Ministry of Commerce have been trying to encourage people to have a high level of awareness of the behavior and how to use alcohol properly and provide effective solutions.
"From my own point of view, I can see that people in developed and developing countries are consuming alcohol, but the behavior or consumption of consumers depends on the knowledge and education in that country," he said. " Mr. Phan Oun claimed that today, the launch of the responsible alcohol campaign is moving in the right direction and in line with the government's agenda towards sustainable growth.
Delegates also expressed their enthusiasm and cooperation with the private sector for initiatives with the common goal of developing a better and more dynamic country. At the same time, Mr. Som Ratana supported this campaign, which focuses on education because it is the foundation of development and growth. He said the campaign is not only beneficial for the individual, but also for the nation as a whole.
The Undersecretary of State believes that raising awareness of alcohol consumption will truly benefit young people who play a key role in sustainable development, and that young people will be involved in supporting and disseminating the campaign more widely.
Mr. Nuon Pharath praised Cambrew Initiative, which is an important initiative and will also benefit and support the Royal Government's efforts to raise public awareness regarding alcohol consumption. Especially in the road safety campaign.
"We would like to see the entire alcohol industry work together with the Royal Government to promote and raise awareness about responsible drinking, as well as: to actively participate in addressing drug-related challenges," he said. Drunk. We must unite for the same purpose, for the benefit of our society as a whole.
"However, irresponsible drinking can turn those positive moments into negative ones," he said. "From Cambrew, a responsible brewery, we want to see consumers enjoy a fair amount of alcohol and lead by example in responsible drinking."
"No irresponsible drinking" has been incorporated into its business strategy, which includes both specific goals and action plans and is part of the company's sustainability objectives, said Cambrew. "Develop better today and tomorrow."
