Lim Ranout is a child who was raised by his parents in a rural area in Kampi village, Sambok commune, Chit Borey district, Kratie province. Despite poor family life, growing up free of disease keeps Lim Ranout happy with his family.
Unfortunately, for the last two years, Ranut has been suffering from a disease that causes him constant headaches. When she was sick, Ranut's mother used to take painkillers. The first symptoms of the disease, when taking the medicine, the pain disappears. Taking the medicine only relieves the pain for a short time, and soon the symptoms return. Ranut's mother does the same thing every time her child has a headache.
For the last half month or so, the painkillers were no longer effective and the symptoms worsened, causing Ranut's head to ache and his left eye to become numb and stiff.
After ingestion, the drug is no longer effective. Ranut's mother sent him to Kantha Bopha Hospital, a free humanitarian hospital, in early June.
Upon receiving the results of the examination by a team of doctors that Ranout had a brain tumor and a large tumor, the mother and Ranout were stunned and lost consciousness.
Whether life is a natural or inevitable disease, all human life on the planet longs for a long family reunion. The result of the disease diagnosed by the doctor was a life-threatening illness, which made Ranut and his mother very worried and anxious.
Fortunately for Lim Ranout, who is currently at Kantha Bopha Hospital, a specialist doctor with state-of-the-art medical equipment can provide services for brain tumor surgery. After a short stay in the hospital on June 13, Koranut underwent surgery by a team of doctors at Kantha Bopha Hospital with the utmost care.
Dr. Kong Vuthy, a neurosurgeon, has been working at Kantha Bopha Hospital for more than 10 years. "Neurosurgery is a difficult operation," he said.
Doctors claim that surgery for Ranut's brain tumor took up to eight hours. This surgery is not able to rest, eat or go to the bathroom, that is, when the surgery is complete. Although it took a long time, Dr. Kong Vuthy and the rest of the surgeons were pleased that the operation was successful, removing the tumor from the brain and not affecting other organs.
After about a week of surgery, Ranut recovered. The grief of Lim Ranout's parents and family has returned to normal after a large tumor in his brain was successfully removed.
Ms. So Srey Mom as well as Ly Ranout expressed her deep gratitude to all the leaders and doctors of Kantha Bopha for their efforts to save Ranut's life from the deadly disease and help Ranuth to continue living with his parents and See this beautiful planet go on.
Although the tumor was surgically removed from his brain, Lim Ranout continued to receive treatment to ensure that his tumor did not grow back. This continued treatment because the tumor removed from the brain was found to be "malignant" required further treatment with chemotherapy and other techniques. Tomorrow morning, the National Assembly will hold a meeting to review and discuss the draft law on the amendment of the election law.
